                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1018-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1018-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1018-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Telefunken Elektroakustik Ela M 251T</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Tube Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Ela M 251 T is a variation on Telefunken Elektroakustik&#8217;s reissue of the reknowned Telefunken Ela M 251E, and is identical to the reissued 251E in all respects save two: the body color and the capsule.</p><p>Telefunken Electroakustik has gone to considerable expense to recreate the AKG CK-12 capsule; see the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-251E">ElaM 251E</a> profile for more information. In 2009, the company announced the development of a titanium/aluminum alloy called Alloy 2213, which when used in place of the gold-coated Mylar of their CK-12/TK-12 capsules yielded a sound described as &#8220;airy,&#8221; &#8220;delicate,&#8221; and &#8220;more detailed.&#8221;</p><p><a href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/telefunkenusa/elam_251e_circuit.jpg" rel="shadowbox"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/telefunkenusa/elam_251e_circuit-sm.jpg" width="200" alt="Telefunken USA Ela M 251E Circuit" class="thumb shaft" /></a>The Ela M 251 T retains the GE JAN 6072a tube, the Haufe transformer, and the modular assembly technique of the 251E. Note that the capsule pictured at right is the gold-diaphragm CK-12, not the titanium-alloy CK-13; the circuit is otherwise accurate.</p><p>The 251T ships with a wooden storage case for the mic, a historically-accurate power supply, a power cable, and a vintage suitcase.</p><p>For context, see Telefunken Elektroakustik&#8217;s entire line of ElaM 250/251 microphones:</p><table><tr><td><b>Model</b></td><td><b>Capsule</b></td><td><b>Tube</b></td><td><b>Patterns</b></td><td><b>PSU</b></td><td style="text-align:right"><b>MSRP (2010)</b></td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/Ela-M-250">Ela M 250</a></td><td>CK-12</td><td>AC-701</td><td>Cardioid, Omni</td><td>Historical</td><td style="text-align:right">$13,495</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/Ela-M-251">Ela M 251</a></td><td>CK-12</td><td>AC-701</td><td>Cardioid, Omni, Figure-8</td><td>Historical</td><td style="text-align:right">$13,995</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-250E">Ela M 250 E</a></td><td>CK-12</td><td>6072a</td><td>Cardioid, Omni</td><td>Historical</td><td style="text-align:right">$10,495</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-251E">Ela M 251 E</a></td><td>CK-12</td><td>6072a</td><td>Cardioid, Omni, Figure-8</td><td>Historical</td><td style="text-align:right">$10,995</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-250F">Ela M 250 F</a></td><td>TK-12</td><td>6072a</td><td>Cardioid, Omni</td><td>Modern</td><td style="text-align:right">n/a</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-251F">Ela M 251 F</a></td><td>TK-12</td><td>6072a</td><td>Cardioid, Omni, Figure-8</td><td>Modern</td><td style="text-align:right">($5,995)</td></tr><tr><td><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-251T">Ela M 251 T</a></td><td>CK-13</td><td>6072a</td><td>Cardioid, Omni, Figure-8</td><td>Historical</td><td style="text-align:right">n/a</td></tr></table><p>(MSRP current as of Nov, 2010)<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Telefunken-USA/ElaM-251T">Telefunken Elektroakustik ElaM-251T</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2009.</p>
